+# Local patches to the vendored TMS34010_sv core
+
+The core is vendored from `birdybro/TMS34010_sv` (MIT, commit 39c7aaf). Keep
+patches here small, documented, and upstreamable so `git pull` conflicts stay
+localized. Prefer proposing them upstream.
+
+Toolchain: the integrated core sim needs **Questa** (birdybro's toolchain). The
+core uses SV struct assignment patterns (`'{n:…,c:…,z:…,v:…}`) and `unsigned'()`
+casts (e.g. core.sv:1503–2370) that **Icarus 12 cannot parse**. Icarus is fine
+for the Icarus-clean Y-unit wrapper unit tests (`sim/`), not for the core.
+
+---
+
+## P0001 — reset-vector read (REQUIRED to boot a real game)
+
+**Status:** ✅ APPLIED 2026-07-03 (3 edits, marked `P0001` in-source). Verify with
+Questa via `sim/tb_yunit_boot.sv` (Icarus can't compile the core). Applied edits:
+- `rtl/tms34010_pkg.sv` — added `CORE_RESET_VEC = 6'd38` to `core_state_t`.
+- `rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv` (mem-interface comb) — `CORE_RESET` now `→ CORE_RESET_VEC`;
+  new `CORE_RESET_VEC` reads `mem[TRAP_VECTOR_BASE]` (`0xFFFFFFE0`, 32-bit), `→ CORE_FETCH` on ack.
+- `rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv` (pc-load comb) — on `CORE_RESET_VEC && mem_ack`,
+  `pc_load_en=1; pc_load_value=mem_rdata_eff` (loads PC = the vector word).
+To re-apply after `git pull`: `grep -n P0001 rtl/**/*.sv` shows all three sites.
+
+**Problem.** `CORE_RESET` (core.sv:2450) transitions straight to `CORE_FETCH`
+with `pc = RESET_PC = '0` (pkg RESET_PC is flagged a placeholder). Real TMS34010
+hardware, on reset, loads the PC from the 32-bit long-word at the level-0 trap
+(reset) vector `0xFFFFFFE0` and begins execution there.
+
+**Evidence.** Smash T.V. rev 8.00: the reset long-word at bit-addr `0xFFFFFFE0`
+= `0xFFE0F5C0` (measured from the program ROM; the ROM tail is a clean
+`0xFFE0xxxx` vector table). With `RESET_PC=0` the core fetches garbage at 0 and
+never boots. Confirmed via `yunit_mem` (a 32b read @0xFFFFFFE0 returns
+`0xFFE0F5C0`).
+
+**Fix.** Model reset like the existing interrupt-vector read (`CORE_INT_VECTOR`,
+core.sv:2497), which already does exactly this shape:
+
+```
+mem_req  = 1'b1;
+mem_addr = <vector>;          // here: 32'hFFFF_FFE0  (TRAP_VECTOR_BASE, trap 0)
+mem_size = MEM_SIZE_32;       // 32-bit
+if (mem_ack) <latch mem_rdata -> PC>;
+```
+
+Concretely, add a `CORE_RESET_VEC` state entered from `CORE_RESET`:
+
+1. `CORE_RESET → CORE_RESET_VEC` (instead of `→ CORE_FETCH`).
+2. `CORE_RESET_VEC`: drive `mem_req=1`, `mem_addr=TRAP_VECTOR_BASE` (`0xFFFFFFE0`),
+   `mem_size=MEM_SIZE_32`; on `mem_ack`, load PC from `mem_rdata` (reuse the
+   `pc_load_value`/`popped_pc_q` path used by `CORE_INT_VECTOR`) and go to
+   `CORE_FETCH`.
+
+This is ~one state + the PC-load reuse — small and localized. It generalizes to
+every T/Y/Z-unit game (each has its own reset vector), which is why it is
+preferred over hardcoding `RESET_PC` per game.
+
+**Verification (Questa).** In `tb_yunit_boot` (to write): core + `yunit_mem` with
+the smashtv ROM; after reset, assert `pc_o == 0xFFE0F5C0` before the first real
+fetch, then that fetches walk into the program (cross-check the first N fetch
+addresses against `mame smashtv -debug`).
+
+**Upstream note.** birdybro flags `RESET_PC` as a placeholder — this is the
+natural next step for their core too. Offer it upstream once verified.
+
+---
+
+## P0002 — hoist forward-referenced declarations (Questa FSE 25.1std compat)
+
+**Status:** ✅ APPLIED 2026-07-03. **Compile-verified** (full design, 0 errors under
+Questa FSE 25.1std / 2025.2). Behavior-neutral; runtime verify pending license (date-gated).
+
+**Problem.** `tms34010_core.sv` references 33 module signals *before* their textual
+declaration (legal SV — module items are order-independent; fine on birdybro's FSE
+25.1.1 and on Icarus/Quartus). **FSE 25.1std (Questa 2025.2) enforces
+declaration-before-use** and rejects them (vlog-2730 "undefined", or vlog-2388 for
+LHS-of-assign cases like `wvp_set`). No vlog flag / modelsim.ini key relaxes it
+(verified: `-permissive`, `-mfcu`, unit-import, `-svinputport=var` — the last fixes a
+*separate* typed-port issue only).
+
+**Fix.** Move the 33 signals' declaration lines verbatim to a `// ===== P0002` block
+right after the module port list (before first use). Pure declarations → order-neutral,
+so no behavior change; the clean compile confirms no syntax/type breakage.
+
+**Re-apply after `git pull`** (regenerates the hoist from a fresh core):
+```
+git -C rtl/tms34010 checkout rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv
+python3 rtl/tms34010/patches/hoist_p0002.py \
+        rtl/tms34010/rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv \
+        rtl/tms34010/patches/p0002_signals.txt
+# then re-apply P0001 (above) — the two P0001 edits target FSM/pc-load blocks the
+# hoist does not touch, so apply order is hoist-then-P0001 or vice-versa.
+```
+The signal list `patches/p0002_signals.txt` is self-converging: if a future upstream
+adds a new forward-ref, the compile prints it (vlog-2730/2388) — append it and re-run.
+
+**Not needed on:** Icarus, Quartus synth, or Questa FSE **25.1.1**. Only 25.1std/2025.2.
+Compile flag required regardless: `vlog -svinputport=var` (see `sim/run_questa.sh`).
+
+---
+
+## P0003 — ANDI immediate is one's-complemented (CORRECTNESS: every ANDI was wrong)
+
+**Status:** ✅ APPLIED + VERIFIED 2026-07-04. One-token core fix + one test-vector fix.
+Marked `P0003` in-source. Re-apply after `git pull`: `grep -rn P0003 rtl/`.
+
+**Applied edits:**
+- `rtl/core/tms34010_decode.sv` (`ANDI_IL_TOP11` block, ~line 691):
+  `decoded.alu_op = ALU_OP_AND;` → **`ALU_OP_ANDN;`**
+- `sim/tb/tb_immi_il.sv` (ANDI vector): the placed immediate `0x0FF0FF0F` →
+  **`0xF00F00F0`** (= `~0x0FF0FF0F`), so the effective mask stays `0x0FF0FF0F`
+  and the expected result `0x00F0F000` is unchanged. The old vector encoded the
+  *wrong* convention (direct mask) and only passed because the core was also wrong.
+
+**Problem.** On the TMS34010 the `ANDI` instruction stores the **one's complement**
+of the mask in its 32-bit immediate field. The TI assembler emits `~K`, and the CPU
+computes `Rd & ~(immediate_field) = Rd & K`. birdybro's decoder set `ALU_OP_AND`
+(`a & b`) with the raw immediate, so it computed `Rd & ~K` — the complement of the
+intended mask. **Every `ANDI` in every game was silently corrupted.** (`ORI`/`XORI`
+store the immediate *directly* and were already correct; the register form `ANDN Rs,Rd`
+was already correct — it uses `ALU_OP_ANDN`.)
+
+**Evidence (real ROM + MAME, authoritative).** Smash T.V. boot RAM-test field-size
+setup: `MOVE A11,A9 (=0x10); ANDI 1Fh,A9; EXGF A9,1` (sets FS1 from the stride).
+- The ROM encodes "ANDI 1Fh" as raw immediate **`0xFFFFFFE0`** (`= ~0x1F`); both
+  `unidasm -arch tms34010` and MAME disassemble it as `ANDI 1Fh`.
+- **MAME** (`mame smashtv -debug`, trace with `tracelog A9`): after `ANDI 1Fh` on
+  `A9=0x10`, **A9 = 0x10** (mask `0x1F` applied). → complement model, definitive.
+- **This core, pre-fix** (Questa `tb_yunit_fsprobe`): `A9 = 0x10 & 0xFFFFFFE0 = 0`,
+  so `EXGF` loaded **FS1=0** (=32-bit fields). The palette RAM test then filled with
+  a **32-bit** stride where MAME uses **16-bit** — the first *observable* symptom.
+- **This core, post-fix:** `A9=0x10` → `FS1=16` → palette stride **16**, byte-exact
+  with MAME. `tb_immi_il` PASS, `tb_yunit_boot` PASS, RAM-test descriptor march
+  (`tb_yunit_ramtest`) matches MAME's golden list on start-addr + count + stride.
+
+**Why boot appeared to "work" before:** wrong `ANDI` results produced wrong *data*,
+not illegal opcodes, so PC never derailed. The RAM test even "progressed" because
+fill and verify used the same (wrong-but-consistent) field size. The bug only became
+visible by diffing field *stride* against MAME.
+
+**Upstream note.** This is a real core bug (not Y-unit-specific) — offer upstream to
+birdybro along with the corrected `tb_immi_il` vector. Spec: TMS34010 User's Guide,
+`ANDI` (the immediate is stored complemented). MAME `tms34010` executes the same.
